  <h2 id="about-the-author">About the Author</h2>

  <p>The book's back cover, closing where the front cover opened it:</p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image source-doc-image--compact">
    <img src="/images/site/once-around-back-cover-author-bio.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of John Richard Jackson dressed as an old-time miner, wearing a wide-brimmed hat and a bandana and smoking a cigarette, from the back cover of &quot;Once Around the Thunder Mug.&quot;">
    <figcaption>From the back cover of "Once Around the Thunder Mug," 1993.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <blockquote>
    <p>John Richard Jackson had a rewarding 27 years "in the hole." He began work as a mucker operating a #6 square point shovel for which he was both physically and mentally qualified. He rose in rank to become both manager and independent operator of several small hard rock mines, positions for which he was eminently unqualified and he promptly went broke; several times. The last 13 years of his working career were spent with Freeport Exploration Company prospecting for precious metals where the tools were advanced geology and geophysics, a far cry from his first shift in the mines of Creede. He retired in 1989 and now lives on a small ranch in Hinsdale County with his wife, Inez, and their Siberian Husky, Skeeter.</p>
  </blockquote>

  <p>Written in the third person, as back-cover author notes are, but almost certainly in John's own words. "Now lives" reflects the book's 1993 printing; see <a href="/people/john-richard-jackson/">John's own page</a> for what became of him afterward.</p>
</section>
